A||||13||fierospeeder||People who complain to protect their kids||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||09-24-2001||02:57 AM||||There is probably a lot of things that people complain about.  But, I will bring one issue which Ive been reading about and witnessed and it pisses me off.<P><BR>I work at a grocery store part time/ part time student, and a stripper  of car parts.<BR>Anyway, a lady comes in 2 weeks ago, and tosses a "Instyle" magazine at me and asks me why is this up near the registers.  <P>She wants to talk to a manager because she thinks its inappropriate for a kid to be seeing something like that.  (the front cover had a lady that was nude, she was sitting on her ass, with her knees against her chest, and her arms wrapped around her legs, and the image is her side is facing us.  You CANNOT see her nipples or krotch(sp) or even a buttcrack)<P>Our grocerystore has a independent magazine company that does the magazine stand.  Eventhough she complained.  The people in charge in nonfood dept. took down the magazines.  Yes cosmopolitin is still in the front with the 20 sex positions.  Woohoo.<P>Nextweek, this lady comes up and throws two magazines at me.  i just wanted to f deck her.  She asks what are these doing here.  She explains that there is a catholic school across the street, which is across a busy street, 4 lanes.  And she doesn't want her 14 year old son to be looking at this stuff.  And if My store, doesn't comply with taking down the mag, she threatens to get a local newspaper to write an article.  "ironic way of freedom of press?"<BR>I explained to her, i did remember her, and they did take the magazines down last week.  And we got someone to write a note to the nonfood dept. manager. yada yada.<P>Explanation, yes someone forgot to take 4 magazines down last week, instyle of course.  And the other magazine.  Pamela Anderson special, with posters in it, that a customer was reading, i mean viewing and left it on the magazine stand, like other food products that people dont want.  Our store is guilty that a customer left a magazine there.   When the lady left, few kids were eyeing some of those female magazines,  YES FEMALE MAGAZINES.<P><BR>2nd story and last.  I finally subscribe to several car/truck magazines instead of paying newstand price.  So i get my "Four Wheeler" magazine thursday.  In the letters, a father complained that he will no longer subscribe to them because there was a lady advertised in the magazine for some product and it offended him.  He lets his son read four wheeler all the time.  And now, this situation has made his son to stop reading four wheeler, and seek another magazine, eventhough hes (dad) been a subscriber for YEARS.   His son, 16 years old.  <P>the magazine just states they dont control the advertisers, and their magazine was bought out, so they dont really have anything to do with it.  I sometimes notice this with swimsuit issues or some models posing on cars and people complain.<P><BR>People complaining about these things just PO me off so much.  Your sons 16, i bet he has the internet and is ____  when your not home.   You can say the human body is art, and i can bring that argument into it.  Wait its only art when they are nude.  Dam im wrong.  I wonder if these people have TVs because theres more nudity on the tv screen.<P>Soon you will have to be 18 to buy any magazine.  I guess there wont any teens reading magazines and dreaming about being shadetree mechs and fixing their cars.<BR>maybe a woodworking mag.<P>Just pisses me off.  <P>i didn't proofread, so ignore my grammer.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Galen||09-24-2001||03:18 AM||||People do that all the time to HotRod..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> One reason why I like the editors at Hot Rod, they dont take the junk from the 'stupid' customers.. "Chicks and cars dont go together" they say, to heck with that.. Women and cars are meant to be together, two beautiful things in one place, who can argue?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/g-flag-sig.gif">  <FONT COLOR="purple">1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe</FONT> - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>Fierolinks</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Urchin||09-24-2001||03:25 AM||||Well, after reading that, two things come to mind.  1.  Lazy parents and 2. Get out more <P>An essential part of parenting is teaching your child about the things you encounter in everyday life. ie. how these things effect you, why they exist, how to deal with them, etc.  It is inevitable that these "children" are going to encounter these "things" (magazines or what have you) at some point.  Wouldn't it be smarter to teach the child about these things rather than sheild them from it.  People like the woman you described are usually the same ones who walk in the door and set the kid in front of the t.v. for the rest of the night.  Too lazy, or too stupid to properly parent a child.  (yes this issue bothers me too)<P>My second point is that if these people think that magazines as you are describing are offensive enough to create a stink about, they really need to get out more.  Magazines like these are obviously common.  There are a heck of a lot worse things to b1tch about.  If I was a parent (which I am not), magazines would be the least of my concerns. I could perhaps understand if it were Playboy, but it wasn't.  These people must lead sheltered lives.<P>Just my thoughts...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/final.jpg"> <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-24-2001||04:02 AM||||I manage a C store so I see a wide variety of monthly rags.  Believe me.  A ton of "mainstream" rags (i.e. Cosmopolitan, etc) have more nudity and sexual discussion than Playboy.  (I sold Playboy for about 4 months until the distributor quit offering it.)  You can't even see the front cover of Playboy, but the front cover of Cosmo (I think--maybe a pink cover?) has very bold lettering saying something about the 30 positions to a better orgasm (something to that effect).  I have an 11 year old son.  I don't care if he sees Playboy (I don't buy it for him, but if he happens to notice it, I won't spaz).  Better that than the real Cosmo type smut.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||09-24-2001||09:19 AM||||you dont need to get mags for this stuff<P>our city newspaper has Dr Ruth columns, explicited discussions of personal sexual issues<P> next time she complains tell here there are places she can take her kids where they wont be exposed to this kind of stuff.<P> A large community near Lancaster Pennsylvania is one.<P>  But then she'll have to give up her car.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 09-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Springtyme||09-24-2001||09:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>Women and cars are meant to be together, two beautiful things in one place, who can argue?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I saw a bumper sticker the other day that said "If it has t!t's or wheels, it will give you problems" Even though I'm a girl, I thought it was kinda funny... hehehe =)<P>(edited cause I screwed up the UBB code)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Springtyme.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Springtyme (edited 09-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||09-24-2001||10:37 AM||||I agree with Urchin, If you censor your kids too much then when they are older they may not be able to deal with other problems later in life. I'm not saying buy a Playboy for your 10 year old, but with T.V., Cable, the internet, and newspapers/magazines. I believe it's better to teach the children to censor themselves. Give them the responsibility of learning self censorship. Let them decide what they want or don't want to see. Generally if you tell someone they can't look at it and make a huge deal about it, the more they want it. It goes along the lines of the difference between the 2 signs on a wall. If a sign says "wet paint" nobody touches it. But if you put up a sign that says "DO NOT TOUCH" odds are a large percenatge of the people will touch it. I guess that is the natural rebel inside all humans.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||frontal lobe||09-24-2001||11:30 AM||||I don't blame you for being mad at the woman, because she was being obnoxious.<P>There are many people, myself included, that want to protect our children from exposure to many topics, including nudity, hyper-sexuality, etc.  At home that is easier to control--NO television.  I mean none.  They don't get to watch any.  And it means I can't even sit and watch 1/2 hour of football because of the commercials or cheerleaders.  It's much harder to control out in the world-billboards, magazines in grocery stores, etc.  <P>I wanted to just make a couple of points really clear:<P>1.  I will make sure my kids are educated about these things but I just wish I could be the one to decide WHEN to bring up 30 ways to a better orgasm with my daughters than having them ask me what an orgasm is in the checkout line at a grocery store when they are 6 years old (youngest) or 10 years old (oldest).  There are MANY things in life that we educate ourselves on at an appropriate age and state of development.<P>2.  There is no excuse to be "in people's face" about it.  If you feel that strongly about it, kindly ask to talk to the manager and rationally explain why you feel that way and is there anything that could be done about it.  If no, consider going to different stores where it isn't as much of a factor.<P>Finally, I HATE bumper stickers every since my children learned to read.  Dad, what does 1-800-KISS-@!% mean?  What does eat ##** mean?  <P>Anyway, getting long here but my main point is that I am sorry that some hag may have had a reasonable motive but abused you about it.  No excuse for that.  None.  Zero.||||reg||1
Z||000008||annieL||09-24-2001||06:47 PM||||As a Mom I know I wouldn't of complained. If the lady didn't like it she should shop elsewhere. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||LarryB||09-24-2001||06:49 PM||||Hey, if you want to see get-a-lifers in action read the letters that are published to the editor that Sports Illustrated gets every year after their swimsuit issue comes out. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick's Dad||09-25-2001||10:06 AM||||Agreeing with Frontal Lobe:<P>Too many subject to list are the ones that we, as parents, would like to set the time and tone of how we discuss them with our children.<P>With freedom comes responsibility. Unfortunately, too many people have forgotten that. If T&A is the community standard, then what must your community be like? Yes, I've read Hot Rod (Until I found Kit Car. More useful info, though very Cobra heavy....), and I subscribed to two photo magazines that I gave up because they were more self service to a bunch of "inner circle" types then to photography itself. Yeah, there were boobs in there, and an occasional butt, but they weren't where my kid could find them. I also found a better mag that I didn't have to hide. Unfortuantely, in the three major supermarket chains that are in my area (as well as WalMart) Cosmo, InStyle, and Glamour are right at the checkout. The point is, they are businesses that cater to the family and they sould act it.<P>The company I work for (A camera store) carries books on nude photography. One person asked me about what I tought about nude photography, I told him that I didn't feel comfortable getting undressed to take pictures. Another complained to me that it was too difficult to reach them, and why did we put them up so high? as he was thumbing through one. As he stopped at one image, I simply said "Those."<P>In simple terms, we are free to carry them, but we are responsible enough to keep them out of kids' sight and reach. Maybe we should let the markets know that we'd rather see the Disney magazine on the rack.||||reg||1
